<section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="no-script-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="no-script-title">Without JavaScript</h2>
    <p>
      The components send finished HTML, so the pages can be read before any script runs, or if none runs. With
      scripting off, a media query in the stylesheet turns the stack into a scroll-snap row, with the pages under it
      drawn as two edges, and the book into a row of spreads. Chrome adds arrows and dots with <code>::scroll-button()</code> and <code>::scroll-marker</code>. Other browsers show the edge of the next page.
    </p>
    <p>The frame below is a page in a sandbox that doesn't allow scripts, so it is what a reader with JavaScript off gets.</p>
  </div>

  <iframe
    class="no-script-frame sheet-paper"
    src="/no-script/"
    sandbox="allow-same-origin"
    loading="lazy"
    title="A stack and a book with scripting off"></iframe>
</section>

<style>
  .no-script-frame {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 32rem;
    border: 0;
    background: var(--mat);
    outline: 1px dashed var(--mat-ink-soft);
    outline-offset: 6px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .no-script-frame {
      height: 31rem;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .no-script-frame {
      height: 34rem;
    }
  }
</style>
